{number: 98}
{title: Beneath the Cross of Jesus}
{key: Db}

{start_of_verse: 1}
Be-[Db] neath the cross of [Gb] Jesus I [Db] fain would take my [Ab7] stand,
The [Db] shadow of a [Gb] mighty rock with-[Db] in a [Ab7] weary [Db] land;
A [Gb] home within the [Db] wilderness, a [Ab7] rest upon the [Db] way,
From the [Db] burning of the [Gb] noontide heat, and the [Db] burden [Ab7] of the [Db] day.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 2}
Up-[Db] on that cross of [Gb] Jesus mine [Db] eye at times can [Ab7] see
The [Db] very dying [Gb] form of One Who [Db] suffered [Ab7] there for [Db] me;
And [Gb] from my stricken [Db] heart with tears two [Ab7] wonders I con-[Db] fess:
The [Db] wonders of re-[Gb] deeming love, and [Db] my un-[Ab7] worthi-[Db] ness.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 3}
I [Db] take, O cross, thy [Gb] shadow for [Db] my abiding [Ab7] place;
I [Db] ask no other [Gb] sunshine than the [Db] sunshine [Ab7] of His [Db] face;
Con-[Gb] tent to let the [Db] world go by, to [Ab7] know no gain nor [Db] loss,
My [Db] sinful self my [Gb] only shame, my [Db] glory [Ab7] all the [Db] cross.
{end_of_verse}
